# Apps — finding one, installing it, updating it

One node publishes an app; **any** node can find it by typing part of its name,
read what it says about itself, download it to look, install it and watch it for
new versions.

## There is no store

There was one, and removing it is the point of this page. A store is a **list**,
and a list on a decentralised mesh is a thing to fill: publishing costs an
attacker nothing an attacker does not already have — a release is signed by the
key it names, and any node can sign its own. Keeping such a list clean is a
moderation problem, and moderation needs an authority nobody on this network is
in a position to be. Every defence the catalogue grew (a per-author share,
trusted-first eviction) is a way of losing that fight more slowly.

So nothing is listed. Finding an app works exactly the way finding a person by
name works: you **ask** for a name, and signed records filed under it come back
from the [package directory](../Updates/guide). Nothing to enumerate, nothing to
flood, and the same view, the same page and the same three buttons as a node
release — because it is the same problem twice.

Modules: [`src/pkg_dir.py`](../../src/pkg_dir.py) (the directory record and its
keys), [`src/app_package.py`](../../src/app_package.py) (content-addressed
packages, signed releases), [`src/app_catalog.py`](../../src/app_catalog.py)
(the installed registry, and the older gossiped catalogue), the package section
of `src/node.py`.

## Finding one

Console → **Apps → Find an app**. Typing searches what this node already holds;
**Ask the network** spends one bounded round of directory queries. Opening a
result shows the package page (`/package#<id>`): name, version, author, the few
signed lines the author wrote, how many publishers have signed the same code,
and the three things you can do — **download** it to open the archive by hand,
**install** it, **watch** it for new versions.

That is the same page a node release opens, and deliberately: an operator should
not have to learn two ways to look at a signed package.

## Two layers

- **The directory** (network-wide, asked rather than listed) — a signed record
  per published app, filed under its author's publisher id and under its name's
  prefixes. See [`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide) for the record format.
- **Installed** (local, persisted) — what *this* node has actually installed
  (fetched + written to disk), distinct from what exists on the network.

The older gossiped **catalogue** is still there and still fills from
`CATALOG_ANNOUNCE`: a node that already holds entries should not lose what it
knows, and a release descriptor pulled through the directory is offered to it so
the installer can read it. Nothing is published *in order to be listed* any
more, which is what took the flooding problem off the table.

## Propagation (bounded epidemic gossip)

A packet type `CATALOG_ANNOUNCE` (0x18) carries a signed release between
**direct authenticated** peers (like `STORE`). On receipt the node:

1. **verifies** the signature (reject by default if it is invalid);
2. accepts it only if the `app_id` is unknown **or** the `ts` is strictly higher
   than what it holds — otherwise it **ignores** it (anti-rollback: a relay
   cannot walk it backwards by replaying an old signed release);
3. if its view changed, **re-announces** to its other peers (the src is
   re-stamped at each hop). Since a duplicate or older entry is not
   re-announced, **the epidemic stops on its own** (no loop, no amplification).

At the end of a handshake, a node **pushes its whole catalogue** to the new peer
(`_sync_catalog_to`): a node that joins catches up on apps published before it
arrived. The rate is bounded per link (`_CATALOG_RATE_MAX`/window), the
catalogue is capped (`MAX_APPS`), and a release is ≤ one DHT value.

## Security (reminders)

- A catalogue entry is **unforgeable** without the author's key (signature) and
  **cannot be walked back** (`ts`). `app_id = deployed_id(author, name)`: nobody
  else publishes under that `app_id`.
- **Install verifies the content** before writing anything: the files are
  reassembled from the DHT and compared against the signed root (content
  addressing). Manifest paths are **sanitised** (`_safe_rel`) → nothing is ever
  written outside the app's folder.
- Everything is **bounded**; a corrupt registry file yields an empty registry,
  never a crash.

## Node API

```python
# Publishes, announces, and files a record under the app's name. `notes` is the
# few lines anybody sees before they fetch anything, so it travels signed.
info = await node.publish_store_app(name, version, files, notes="what it is")

await node.search_packages("sketch")   # ask the directory (local + network)
node.find_packages("sketch")           # …local only: instant, free, search-as-you-type
await node.packages_of(node_id)        # what that node publishes or recommends
node.package_entry(record_id_hex)      # one record, as a page reads it

await node.install_package(record_id_hex)   # fetch + verify + install
await node.fetch_package(record_id_hex)     # (entry, blob, filename) — the bytes, verified
node.subscribe_package(record_id_hex, auto=False, quorum=1)
node.unsubscribe_package(ident_hex)    # the record id, or the subscription's own

node.installed_list()                  # apps installed locally
node.uninstall_app(app_id_hex)         # drops the record + the files → bool
```

**Every decision is made in Python**: a record arrives with its `trusted`,
`attesters` (every publisher of this same code), `agreeing`/`needed` (of those,
the keys this operator chose, and how many the quorum asks for) and
`subscription` — the one watching this **package**, so every copy of it reads
back as watched. The front end only **renders** that view.

## Console API (Bearer/cookie auth)

The package routes are shared with node releases and are listed in
[`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide#console-api): `/api/packages` to ask,
`/api/packages/<id>` to read, `…/download` to fetch the archive,
`…/install`, `…/trust` and `…/subscribe` to act.

What remains here is the installed set and publishing:

| Method | Route | Role |
|---|---|---|
| GET  | `/api/store`            | the local view (`{catalog, installed}`) |
| POST | `/api/store/publish`    | `{name, version, notes?, files:{path:b64}}` → publish and file it |
| POST | `/api/store/install`    | `{app_id}` → install an app already in the catalogue |
| POST | `/api/store/update`     | `{app_id}` → update if newer |
| POST | `/api/store/uninstall`  | `{app_id}` → uninstall |

## Wiring

`scripts/nmesh_node.py` passes `app_store_dir=<data>/appstore` when `--data` is
given (an `installed.json` registry + files under `apps/`). Without `--data`,
the catalogue stays in memory and nothing is installed on disk.
